---
/**
 * The children's privacy policy text: parent-managed notebooks for children
 * under 13. Edit the wording here; the date lives in lib/legal.ts.
 *
 * It has to agree with the in-app parent notice (harvous repo,
 * spa/src/pages/family/ChildPrivacyNotice.tsx, CHILD_NOTICE_VERSION below)
 * and cover docs/privacy/CHILD_PRIVACY_PUBLICATION_CHECKLIST.md. A change
 * that touches what we collect, use or share is a material change: bump the
 * notice version in the app, which pauses notebooks until parents agree again.
 *
 * Bracketed placeholders (.lg-placeholder) are facts still to be supplied
 * and checked by counsel. None may remain when this page is published.
 */
import { CHILD_NOTICE_VERSION } from "../../lib/legal.ts";

const SUPPORT_EMAIL = "derek@harvous.com";
---

<div class="prose-letter space-y-8">
  <div>
    <h2>Who this policy is for</h2>
    <p>
      Harvous is a Bible study notes app run by Testament Made LLC ("we," "us"). Most Harvous
      accounts are for people 13 and older. This policy covers one exception: a notebook that a
      parent or legal guardian in the United States sets up and manages for a child under 13.
    </p>
    <p>
      It explains what we collect from and about your child, why, who can see it, how long we keep
      it, and what you can do about it. Our <a href="/privacy/">main privacy policy</a> covers your
      own Harvous account. Where the two differ for your child's notebook, this one applies.
    </p>
    <p>
      Child notebooks are available only in the United States. You'll see a short version of this
      policy in Harvous before you agree to anything (notice version {CHILD_NOTICE_VERSION}). The
      two say the same thing.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2>Who to contact</h2>
    <p>
      Testament Made LLC operates Harvous and is responsible for your child's information. For any
      question or request about your child's notebook, reach us at:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Email: <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a></li>
      <li>Mail: Testament Made LLC, 2190 SE Tahoe Ln, Waukee, IA 50263</li>
      <li>Phone: <a href="tel:+15158021628">515-802-1628</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2>The companies that help us run it</h2>
    <p>
      These service providers handle information on our behalf, only to run and protect the
      notebook. None of them may use your child's information for their own purposes. We don't
      disclose your child's information to third parties, so there's nothing separate to agree to.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Fly.io</strong> hosts the notebook service.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cloudflare</strong> delivers the notebook's pages and protects it from abuse. Like
        any website host, it sees technical information such as IP addresses.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Supabase</strong> stores the notebook's data and its backups.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Clerk</strong> handles your sign-in to your own Harvous account. Your child does
        not get a Clerk account.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Kids Web Services (KWS)</strong>, run by Epic Games, checks that you are an adult
        before you can set up a notebook. More on that below.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <span class="lg-placeholder">[Placeholder: confirm each provider's contract covers child data, and whether KWS's own verification processors need to be named here.]</span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2>What we collect, and when</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Before you agree.</strong> We collect nothing about your child. To start, we use your
      own Harvous account and email address. When you ask to set up a notebook, we create a
      verification request and send KWS your account email, your language, and that you're in the
      US. KWS checks that you're an adult. Depending on the method you pick, KWS may ask you for a
      payment card or identifying details, such as a Social Security number. We don't receive
      those details. We get only the result. KWS may remember that you're verified (it calls this
      AgeGraph) under its own privacy policy.
      If you don't agree, we won't create a notebook or collect anything from your child.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>When you agree.</strong> After verification, you confirm that you are the child's
      parent or legal guardian, that they're under 13, and that you live in the US. You agree to
      the notice and give a first name or nickname for the notebook. We store that name, your
      confirmations, a link to your account, and a record of your consent: which version of the
      notice you agreed to, and when.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>While your child uses the notebook.</strong> We save what your child makes and
      chooses, so you both can come back to it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>notes, folders, tags and Threads, with their Scripture and resource references;</li>
      <li>Family Bible readings you share, and the thoughts your child writes on them;</li>
      <li>My Bible highlights and verse stickers;</li>
      <li>
        Look back progress: which notes and verses come back, when, and how each answer went;
      </li>
      <li>
        notebook preferences, such as the cover, light or dark mode, text size, and sound and
        motion;
      </li>
      <li>the ministry channels you've approved, and the permissions you've set;</li>
      <li>the last time the notebook was used.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      We don't ask for, and the notebook has no place for, your child's email address, phone
      number, full birth date, photo, or home address. Notes are plain text, with no uploads.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Automatically.</strong> Opening the notebook sends technical information, such as an
      IP address and browser details, to deliver pages and keep the service secure. Temporary
      sign-in information lets your child open the notebook. The link you make works once and
      expires after five minutes, and a session ends after eight hours. IP addresses and the
      session token are persistent identifiers. We use them only to deliver the notebook and keep
      it secure, never to contact your child, build a profile, or advertise.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2>How we use it</h2>
    <p>Only to run the notebook for your child and you:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>to create and manage the notebook, and to show you and your child what's in it;</li>
      <li>to search your child's own notes and readings;</li>
      <li>
        to choose what Look back shows next (Look back's progress is used for nothing else);
      </li>
      <li>to find notebooks that have gone unused, so we can delete them;</li>
      <li>to keep the notebook working, secure, and free of abuse.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2>What we never do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>We don't show ads in a child's notebook or use your child's information for advertising.</li>
      <li>We don't sell or rent your child's information.</li>
      <li>We don't track your child across sites or build a profile to target them.</li>
      <li>
        We don't run optional analytics or session recording on the notebook. Optional analytics in
        your own account don't receive your child's notebook or its content.
      </li>
      <li>
        Nothing in a child's notebook is public. There's no public sharing, no direct messages, no
        way to find other children, and no purchases.
      </li>
      <li>
        We don't send your child's information to AI services, and AI connectors aren't available
        for child notebooks.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      We share your child's information only with the providers listed above, to run the notebook,
      or where the law requires it or it's needed to protect someone's safety or the security of
      the service. You can agree to the notebook without agreeing to any other sharing, because we
      don't ask for any.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2>Who can see what</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>You</strong> can read and export everything in your child's notebook.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Family Bible thoughts</strong> are seen by your child and you, the parent managing
        the notebook. Removing a shared reading deletes its thoughts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Verse stickers</strong> are private to your child in the app, but they're included
        when you export the notebook.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ministry leaders and other members</strong> can't read your child's notebook or
        find their profile. If you approve a ministry channel you follow, your child can read it,
        but can't post or reply. Their name never appears in its member list. If you leave the
        channel or remove your approval, their access ends.
      </li>
      <li><strong>No one else</strong> sees your child's notebook. Nothing in it is public.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <span class="lg-placeholder">[Placeholder: when, if ever, Testament Made staff may open a child's notebook, such as to fix a problem a parent reports.]</span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2>How we keep it safe</h2>
    <p>
      The child notebook runs on its own web address, apart from adult Harvous, and only our server
      can read its data. Sign-in links are single-use and short-lived, and they stay out of server
      logs. Your child can't open adult Harvous from the notebook.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2>Your rights as a parent</h2>
    <p>You can, at any time:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Review</strong> your child's notes, readings, and thoughts.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Export</strong> the notebook as a readable text file or as a full backup file. Your
        child can ask for an export from their notebook, but you choose it and download it.
      </li>
      <li><strong>End sessions</strong>, so the notebook has to be opened again with a new link.</li>
      <li><strong>Remove access</strong> to any ministry channel you approved.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Withdraw consent.</strong> Your child's access stops right away, and we stop
        collecting anything new. We keep the notebook for 30 days so you can export it, then delete
        it. A withdrawn notebook can't be turned back on.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Delete</strong> the notebook and everything in it, straight away.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Do all of this in Harvous: sign in, open My Family, then Child notebooks, at
      <a href="https://app.harvous.com/settings/child-accounts">app.harvous.com/settings/child-accounts</a>.
      You can also email, write, or call us using the details above. Before we act on a request
      made outside Harvous, we'll check that it really comes from the parent who manages the
      notebook.
      <span class="lg-placeholder">[Placeholder: how we verify a request made by email, mail, or phone.]</span>
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="lg-placeholder">[Placeholder: what we do if someone else says they are the child's parent or legal guardian, or two guardians disagree.]</span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
    <p>
      We keep each kind of information only as long as the notebook needs it, and nothing is kept
      forever.
    </p>
    <table class="lg-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Information</th>
          <th scope="col">Why we collect it</th>
          <th scope="col">Why we keep it</th>
          <th scope="col">When it's deleted</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Name or nickname, consent record, consent history</th>
          <td data-label="Why we collect it">To create the notebook and show you agreed</td>
          <td data-label="Why we keep it">Needed while the notebook exists</td>
          <td data-label="When it's deleted">With the notebook</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Notes, readings, thoughts, highlights, stickers, preferences</th>
          <td data-label="Why we collect it">They are the notebook</td>
          <td data-label="Why we keep it">So your child and you can come back to them</td>
          <td data-label="When it's deleted">When you delete them, 30 days after you withdraw consent, or after a year unused</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Look back progress</th>
          <td data-label="Why we collect it">To choose what to show next</td>
          <td data-label="Why we keep it">Spacing depends on what came before</td>
          <td data-label="When it's deleted">With the notebook</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Sessions and sign-in links</th>
          <td data-label="Why we collect it">To open the notebook</td>
          <td data-label="Why we keep it">Only while it's open</td>
          <td data-label="When it's deleted">Links after five minutes, sessions after eight hours</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Verification requests</th>
          <td data-label="Why we collect it">To confirm you're an adult</td>
          <td data-label="Why we keep it">Until you finish setting up</td>
          <td data-label="When it's deleted">After seven days, if unfinished</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Database backups</th>
          <td data-label="Why we collect it">To recover from a failure</td>
          <td data-label="Why we keep it">Disaster recovery</td>
          <td data-label="When it's deleted"><span class="lg-placeholder">[Placeholder: backup window. Review found daily backups kept 7 days, so deleted data can stay up to about 8 days.]</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Hosting and security logs, including IP addresses</th>
          <td data-label="Why we collect it">To deliver the notebook and keep it secure</td>
          <td data-label="Why we keep it">To look into abuse</td>
          <td data-label="When it's deleted"><span class="lg-placeholder">[Placeholder: log retention. Review found up to 7 days.]</span></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      A deleted notebook is removed with everything in it, including the consent history. If we
      ever have to restore a backup, we first remove any notebook that was deleted or withdrawn
      after the backup was made.
    </p>
    <p>
      You must delete your child's notebooks before you can delete your own Harvous account.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2>When this policy changes</h2>
    <p>
      If we change what we collect from children, how we use it, or who we share it with, we'll
      ask you to agree again first. Your child's notebook pauses until you read the new notice and
      agree on that notebook's page in My Family. Until then, nothing new is collected. We keep a
      history of each agreement (the version and the date), which you can see in My Family and in
      your export.
    </p>
    <p>
      For smaller changes, like clearer wording or new contact details, we'll update this page and
      its date.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  /* Facts still to be supplied before publication. None may ship. */
  .lg-placeholder {
    padding: 0.05em 0.3em;
    border-radius: 0.3em;
    background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-peach) 45%, var(--color-paper));
    color: var(--color-peach-ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  /* The retention table: four columns on wide screens, one card per row on
     a phone, where each cell shows its column name. */
  .lg-table {
    width: 100%;
    table-layout: fixed;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--color-ink-soft);
  }
  .lg-table th,
  .lg-table td {
    padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem 0.6rem 0;
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
  }
  .lg-table thead th {
    border-top: 0;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    color: var(--color-ink-faint);
  }
  .lg-table tbody th { font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink); }
  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .lg-table thead { display: none; }
    .lg-table tr { display: block; padding: 0.75rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--color-rule); }
    .lg-table th,
    .lg-table td { display: block; padding: 0.15rem 0; border: 0; }
    .lg-table td::before {
      content: attr(data-label);
      display: block;
      font-size: 0.75rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      color: var(--color-ink-faint);
    }
    .lg-table td + td { margin-top: 0.4rem; }
  }
</style>
